it is unclear whether concentration holds at scale `o(ε³n)`.\nWhether `E[pw]/n` converges is a different matter. The standard tool for that,\nthe combinatorial interpolation method of **Bayati, Gamarnik & Tetali (2013)**,\n*Ann. Probab.* 41(6):4080–4115, DOI 10.1214/12-AOP816, arXiv 0912.2444, proves\nlimits for independent set, MAX-CUT, coloring and K-SAT on `G(N, ⌊cN⌋)` and\nrandom regular graphs. It does **not** treat treewidth or pathwidth, and no later\npaper we found does. Its super/subadditivity argument relies on objectives that\nare additive over local constraints. Width parameters are global min–max\nquantities, so the extension is not routine. Related limit results for cut-type\nmin–max objectives exist only for large `c`: Dembo, Montanari & Sen,\n---- EM ----\n62-uniform labelled (Cayley), uniform binary and uniform plane trees, against\n63-the sharp worst case `log₃((6N + 3)/5) ≈ 0.63 log₂ N` of `TreeBound.lean`.\n64-Nothing was found for random recursive trees or binary search trees. **The\n65:most consequential single finding is on the upper side**: Edwards & McDermid\n66-(2015), read in the accepted manuscript, prove\n67-`pw(G) ≤ Σ_{d ≥ 3} g_{1/6}(d) · n_d + o(n)` with an explicit recurrence for\n68-`g_{1/6}`, and on Poisson(`c`) degrees this gives **`pw/n ≤ 0.183, 0.458,\n69-0.656` at `c = 3, 6, 10`**, below every upper bound in the survey's §7(a)\n70-table (0.260, 0.520, 0.760) at every `c` from 2 to about 32, and within a\n71-factor 1.6–1.85 of the fitted slopes. On the clique-cover cells of\n72-`reports/excess_phase_diagram.md` §7 the same bound, evaluated crudely, is\n73-below the project's analysed `β⁺` by 0.05–0.27 at every cell with excess\n74-`e ≤ 3`, the ridge included. Concentration of pathwidth is written nowhere;\n75-Do–Erde–Kang state it for tree- and rank-width in a remark. No theorem on\n76-the node search number or any searching parameter of a random graph was\n77-found (the only random-graph searching result located concerns the *fast*\n78-search number, a different parameter).\n79-\n80----\n81-\n82-## 2. Every statement found\n83-\n84-Constants at `c = 3 / 6 / 10` are on `pw/n` for `G(n, c/n)` unless the model\n85-column says otherwise; \"cubic\" means random 3-regular. \"—\" means the source\n86-says nothing at that parameter or the statement is vacuous there.\n87-\n88-| # | source | model | statement (where) | at c = 3 / 6 / 10, or cubic | held? | label |\n89-|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n90-| 1 | Turner 1986, *SIAM J. Comput.* 15(2):561–580, DOI 10.1137/0215041 | `G(n, p)`, `p` fixed; `p = c ln n / n`; planted `Γ_n(β, p)` | Thm 2.1 `bw > n − 4 log_b n`; Thm 2.2 `bw ≥ (1 − ε) n` for `p = c ln n / n`; Thm 2.3, 3.1, 3.2 and the Conjecture on the planted model (§3.1 below) | — (nothing at `p = c/n`) | text in `reports/scratch/first_moment/lit/turner1986.txt` (main tree) | [pub] |\n--\n103-| 14 | Perarnau & Serra 2014, *DAM* 168:119–126 | random `d`-regular, configuration model | Cor 4.6 linear tw and td for fixed `d ≥ 3`, via `tw ≥ (α(d)(n − 1) − 3)/(α(d) + 3)` with `α(d) = (d − O(√d))/2` from Friedman–Kahn–Szemerédi, constant not made explicit (pp. 11–12) | implicit | yes | [pub] |\n104-| 15 | Kneis, Mölle, Richter & Rossmanith 2009, *SIAM J. Discrete Math.* 23(1):407–427, DOI 10.1137/080715482 | every graph | `pw ≤ m/5.769 + O(log n)`; `m/5.217 + 3` with all bags sharing a large common set (abstract, read through Crossref; `13/75 = 1/5.769` confirmed by 17 p. 19) | 0.260 / 0.520 / 0.867; cubic 0.260 | no (closed) | [pub, abstract] |\n105-| 16 | Fomin, Gaspers, Saurabh & Stepanov 2009, *Algorithmica* 54:181–207, DOI 10.1007/s00453-007-9133-3 | every graph, `n > n_ε` | Lemma 1 **`pw ≤ n₃/6 + n₄/3 + 13n₅/30 + 23n₆/45 + n_{≥7} + εn`** (preprint p. 5; the survey's `n_{≥6}` was wrong); Lemma 2 `tw ≤ (m − n)/3 + εn` for connected `G` with `m/n ∈ [1.5, 2]` | 0.196 / 0.605 / 0.926; cubic 1/6 | yes | [pub] |\n106:| 17 | Edwards & McDermid 2015, *Algorithmica* 72(4):940–968, DOI 10.1007/s00453-014-9883-7 | every graph | Cor 5.2 **`pw(G) ≤ Σ_{d ≥ 3} g_{1/6}(d) n_d + o(n)`**, `g_{1/6}(2) = 0`, `g_{1/6}(3) = 1/6`, `n g(n) = (n − 2) g(n − 1) + g(n − 2) + 1`; Thm 5.3 for average degree `d` (connected or `d ≥ 5`) `pw ≤ (g_{1/6}(d) + o(1)) n = (1 − (7/2)/(d + 1) + O(d^{−3})) n`; restates Golovnev–Kutzkov: `pw ≤ (1 − 4ε ln d / d) n` for `d_ε ≤ d ≤ n/(1 + ε)` (pp. 19–20, 6) | **0.183 / 0.458 / 0.656**; cubic 1/6 | fetched (accepted manuscript) | [pub] |\n107-| 18 | Golovnev & Kutzkov 2014, *TCS* 526:18–27, DOI 10.1016/j.tcs.2014.01.010 | every graph of average degree `d ≥ d_ε` | as restated in 17: `pw ≤ (1 − 4ε ln d / d) n`, `d_ε = O(exp(1/ε))` | large `c` only | no | [pub, as restated] |\n108-| 19 | Díaz, Penrose, Petit & Serna 2001, *J. Algorithms* 39:78–116, DOI 10.1006/jagm.2000.1149; read in Petit's thesis (UPC 2001, ALCOM-FT TR-01-157) | RGG, `n` uniform points in `[0, 1]²`, radius `r_n → 0`, `n r_n² / log n → ∞` | thesis Thm 5.13: **`minvs(G_n) / (n r_n) → 1` a.s.**, likewise bandwidth and vertex bisection; Thm 5.10 `minvs ≥ (1 − 6√ε) n r_n` on `ε`-good graphs; the projection layout attains it (pp. 148–152) | RGG: sharp constant 1 | thesis fetched | [pub, thesis version] |\n109-| 20 | Díaz, Penrose, Petit & Serna 2000, *CPC* 9:489–511; Penrose 2000 | RGG subcritical / supercritical, `n r_n² → λ` | thesis Thm 5.17 `vs = Θ(log n / log log n)` for `λ < λ_c`; Thm 5.5 (Penrose) `minvs = Θ(n r_n)` for `λ > λ_c` (pp. 131, 172) | — | thesis fetched; survey R11 | [pub] |\n110-| 21 | Mitsche & Perarnau 2012/2017 | RGG | Remark p. 5: pathwidth has the same order as tw and td \"since the pathwidth is bounded from below by the treewidth and bounded from above by the treedepth\" | — | yes | [pub] |\n111-| 22 | Brandenberger, Devroye & Reddad 2021, *Electron. J. Probab.* 26:1–29, DOI 10.1214/21-EJP678, arXiv 2010.08613 | critical Galton–Watson trees, offspring variance `σ² ∈ (0, ∞)`, conditioned on size `n` | **`H(T_n) / log₂ n → 1/2` in probability** (p. 2 and Thm 4–5), `H` with leaves at 0; covers Cayley, Catalan, plane and `k`-ary trees; `k`-ary register function `~ log₂ log₂ n / log₂(k/2)` | trees | fetched | [pub] |\n112-| 23 | Devroye & Kruszewski 1995, *IPL* 56:95–99, DOI 10.1016/0020-0190(95)00114-R | uniform binary (Catalan) trees | `E S_n = log₄ n + O(1)`; Thm 4 `P(|S_n − log₄ n| > x) ≤ D/4^x` (abstract, p. 95) | trees | fetched | [pub] |\n113-| 24 | Esparza, Luttenberger & Schlund 2014, LATA, LNCS 8370:1–13, DOI 10.1007/978-3-319-04921-2_1 | every tree | \"We can show that for any tree `t`: `pathwidth(t) − 1 ≤ S(t) ≤ 2 · pathwidth(t)`\", `S` the minimum over roots (p. 11; **stated, no proof**) | trees | fetched | [pub, stated without proof] |\n114-| 25 | Fomin & Høie 2006 citing Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994 | every tree on `n ≥ 3` vertices | Thm 3 `pw(T) ≤ log₃ n` (p. 193) | trees | yes | [pub] |\n115-| 26 | Do, Erde & Kang 2024, *JGT* 106:273–295 | `G(n, (1 + ε)/n)` | §5 p. 14: \"For large enough `ε`, the fact that rank- and tree-width are vertex-Lipschitz functions imply that these parameters are tightly concentrated about their mean, for example using the Azuma–Hoeffding inequality … for smaller `ε` it is not clear if these parameters are concentrated in a range of values of size `o(ε³ n)`\" | — | yes | [pub] |\n116-| 27 | Nikoletseas, Raptopoulos & Spirakis 2008, *TCS* 381 | RIG `G(n, m, p)`, `mp = α log n`, `m ≥ n` | Thm 9: greedy independent set `≥ (1 − β) n / (np)`, `(1 − β) n (1 − e^{−b})`, `(1 − β) n` as `np → ∞`, `→ b`, `→ 0` | RIG: `pw ≤ n − α` [derived] | yes | [pub] |\n117-| 28 | Liu, Wang & Xu 2015, *J. Comb. Optim.* 29(3):531–540, DOI 10.1007/s10878-013-9704-y | sparse random `k`-uniform hypergraphs | \"large hypertree width\" whp; threshold and constants not read | — | no | [pub, abstract] |\n118-| 29 | Tavakoli 2012, M.Sc. thesis, Memorial University (hdl 20.500.14783/2131) | `G(n, p)`, `p` fixed | fast search number of almost all graphs: upper `(9/20 + ε) m` (Def. 3.4, §3.3), lower via `s_fast ≥ |V_odd|/2` (Thm 3.9–3.10, OCR unclear); nothing on node or edge search of random graphs | — (different parameter) | fetched | [pub, OCR] |\n119-| 30 | Bollobás 1985, *Random Graphs*, Academic Press, p. 374 | `G(n, p)` | cited by 3 for bandwidth; **not obtained again** (archive.org item `randomgraphs0000boll` is lending-only; Google Books quota exhausted) | — | no | UNVERIFIED |\n120-\n121-Not found, after searching for them specifically: any theorem on the\n122-pathwidth, vertex separation or node search number of random cubic or random\n123-`d`-regular graphs; of random recursive trees, binary search trees or random\n124-tries; of uniform random planar graphs or random planar maps; of preferential\n125-attachment graphs beyond Gao's Theorem 3; any published concentration\n126-statement for pathwidth; any searching-number theorem on `G(n, p)` other than\n127-29; any cavity or replica prediction for a width.\n128-\n129----\n130-\n131-## 3. Notes by topic\n--\n218-   every graph `G` with maximum vertex degree at most three and with\n219-   `|V(G)| > n_ε`, `pw(G) ≤ (1/6 + ε)|V(G)|`\", proved from the Monien–Preis\n220-   bisection (their Theorem 2) and Lemma 4 (`pw ≤ max{|X|, n/3 + 1 + (2/3) log₃ n + 1}`\n221:   for any vertex subset `X` in a graph of maximum degree 3). Edwards–McDermid\n222-   recover it as `g_{1/6}(3) = 1/6` and use it as the base case of their\n223-   reduction; Kneis et al. give `1.5 n / 5.769 = 0.260 n` for cubic graphs,\n224-   worse. **Nothing better than `n/6` is published for *random* cubic\n225-   graphs**, although the random case is where one would expect it: the\n226-   edge bisection of random cubic graphs is at most `0.139822 n` (Lichev–Mitsche\n227-   Thm 1.1) against Monien–Preis's `n/6` worst case, and Fomin–Høie's\n228-   construction turns a bisection of size `b` into a path decomposition of\n229-   width about `b + o(n)` (the proof of Theorem 5 goes through `|∂(V_i)| ≤\n230-   (1/6 + ε/2) n`, i.e. the number of boundary *vertices*, which for cubic\n231-   graphs is at most the number of cut edges). So **`pw(G_{n,3}) ≤ 0.1399 n + o(n)`\n232-   whp [derived: Lichev–Mitsche's bisection fed through Fomin–Høie's\n233-   construction]**, and the non-rigorous Monte Carlo upper bound Lichev–Mitsche\n234-   report would give less. Check before quoting: it needs the Fomin–Høie\n235-   argument with a balanced but not exactly equal bipartition, which Lemma 4\n236-   allows (`|V_i| ≤ n/2 + 1` enters only through the `n/3` term), and\n237-   Lichev–Mitsche's bisection is exact (`||V_1| − |V_2|| ≤ 1`).\n238-2. **Lower, whp.** The survey's `pw(G_{n,3}) ≥ 0.0721 n` from Kolesnik–Wormald's\n239-   `A_3(1/2) = 0.14420` (vertex isoperimetric number at `u = 1/2`, Theorem 1 and\n240-   Table 2, pp. 5 and 24; read) stands; it is the only explicit constant and it\n241-   is derived, not stated by them. The edge-bisection route is weaker: an active\n242-   prefix vertex of a cubic graph has at most 3 suffix neighbours, so\n243-   `pw ≥ bw/3 ≥ 0.0344 n` from Lichev–Mitsche [derived].\n244-3. **Perarnau–Serra Corollary 4.6** (pp. 11–12) gives linear tw for random\n245-   `d`-regular graphs with `tw ≥ (α(d)(n − 1) − 3)/(α(d) + 3)` and\n246-   `α(d) = (d − O(√d))/2` from the Friedman–Kahn–Szemerédi eigenvalue bound;\n--\n270-\n271-For random `d`-regular graphs in general: lower `A_d/2` from Kolesnik–Wormald\n272-(`0.1448, 0.2510, 0.3569` at `d = 4, 6, 10`), upper `g_{1/6}(d)` from\n273:Edwards–McDermid (`1/3, 23/45 = 0.511, 0.685`) [both derived from pub], and no\n274-source states either for pathwidth.\n275-\n276-### 3.3 Degree-based deterministic bounds, read and evaluated\n277-\n278-**Kneis, Mölle, Richter & Rossmanith 2009.** Closed at SIAM, no repository\n279-copy (Unpaywall, Semantic Scholar, CORE checked); the WG 2005 version\n280-(\"Algorithms based on the treewidth of sparse graphs\", LNCS 3787:385–396) is\n281-closed too. The abstract via Crossref: \"We present a bound of `m/5.769 + O(log n)`\n282-on the pathwidth of graphs with `m` edges. Respective path decompositions can\n283-be computed in polynomial time. … If we agree to accept a slightly larger\n284-bound of `m/5.217 + 3`, we even obtain path decompositions with a rather\n285:simple structure: all bags share a large set of common nodes.\" Edwards–McDermid\n286-(p. 19) restate it as `(13/75 + o(1)) m + O(log n)` and attribute the same\n287-`13/75` for *treewidth* to Scott & Sorkin (arXiv cs/0604080, open). `13/75 =\n288-1/5.769`. On `G(n, c/n)`: `c/11.538`.\n289-\n290-**Fomin, Gaspers, Saurabh & Stepanov 2009**, Lemma 1 (preprint p. 5, read):\n291-`pw(G) ≤ n₃/6 + n₄/3 + 13n₅/30 + 23n₆/45 + n_{≥7} + εn` for `n > n_ε`, with a\n292-polynomial-time decomposition. **The survey's §1.4 has the coefficient list\n293-wrong** (`n_{≥6}` with coefficient 1): degree 6 carries `23/45` and only degree\n294-`≥ 7` carries 1. Lemma 2 (p. 6) is a treewidth bound, `tw ≤ (m − n)/3 + εn`\n295-for connected graphs with `m/n ∈ [1.5, 2]`, which on `G(n, c/n)` would read\n296-`(c/2 − 1)/3` for `3 ≤ c ≤ 4` if the giant component were the whole graph; it\n297-is not, and the bound is for treewidth, so it is not entered in the table.\n298-\n299:**Edwards & McDermid 2015** (accepted manuscript, read; the only one of the","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SrkoPhhe6K6FeoxBGp3xtC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
